Let me write the parser carefully:

```rust
/// Minimal dotenv parser: reads `KEY=value` lines from a `.env` file in a
/// single directory (CWD only — no upward walk). Process environment always
/// wins over `.env` values; callers apply that precedence.
fn load_dotenv(dir: &std::path::Path) -> HashMap<String, String> {
    let mut map = HashMap::new();
    let content = match std::fs::read_to_string(dir.join(".env")) {
        Ok(c) => c,
        Err(_) => return map,
    };
    for raw_line in content.lines() {
        let line = raw_line.trim();
        if line.is_empty() || line.starts_with('#') {
            continue;
        }
        // Optional `export ` prefix (common in shell-exported .env files).
        let line = line.strip_prefix("export ").unwrap_or(line).trim_start();
        let Some(eq) = line.find('=') else { continue };
        let key = line[..eq].trim();
        if key.is_empty() || !key.chars().all(|c| c.is_ascii_alphanumeric() || c == '_') {
            continue;
        }
        let raw_value = line[eq + 1..].trim();
        let value = unquote_value(raw_value);
        map.insert(key.to_string(), value);
    }
    map
}
```

Wait — the Safety rule: never use byte indexing on strings. `line[..eq]` — `eq` is from `line.find('=')` which returns a byte index at a char boundary (since `=` is ASCII, the position is at a byte boundary... actually `find` returns the byte index of the first occurrence of the pattern; since '=' is ASCII one byte, the index is at a char boundary — safe). `line[eq + 1..]` — eq+1 is right after '=', also a char boundary. So safe. But the rule says to use is_char_boundary to be safe. Since both `=` positions are ASCII boundaries, slicing is safe. I'll keep it but this is clean.

unquote_value:
```rust
fn unquote_value(v: &str) -> String {
    let v = v.trim();
    if v.len() >= 2 {
        if (v.starts_with('"') && v.ends_with('"')) || (v.starts_with('\'') && v.ends_with('\'')) {
            return v[1..v.len() - 1].to_string();
        }
    }
    v.to_string()
}
```

`v[1..v.len()-1]` — v starts and ends with quote (ASCII), so 1 and len-1 are char boundaries (len-1 is the byte of the closing quote... if the closing quote is at position len-1, then len-1 is a char boundary since it's the start byte of the ' quote char which is 1 byte). Safe.

Note: I won't handle inline comments (`KEY=value # comment`) — task says "ignore comments `#`" which means lines starting with #. Simple is fine.

